wsi: cancelling a wsi's sul_timeout also undecides its close

Upstream f4a6dc71b ("wsi: a decided close stands") and its follow-up
e9008aa7b make lws_set_timeout(wsi, ..., LWS_TO_KILL_ASYNC) set
wsi->close_decided, and ignore every later timeout on that wsi until
lws_sul_wsitimeout_cb() carries the close out and clears the flag.

__lws_wsi_remove_from_sul() cancels sul_timeout but leaves the flag set.
Then nothing is left to carry the decided close out, and every later
close of the wsi by timeout is ignored as well.  The h1 pipeline handoff,
_lws_generic_transaction_completed_active_conn(), does exactly that: it
cancels the retiring leader's suls by hand, hands its connection to the
next queued transaction, then closes the leader with
lws_set_timeout(wsi, 1, LWS_TO_KILL_ASYNC).  If the leader's close was
already decided, eg, its user aborted it with LWS_TO_KILL_ASYNC from
inside its final RECEIVE_CLIENT_HTTP_READ just before the transaction
completed, that kill is ignored.  The retired leader is left with no
socket and no timeout, and is freed only when the context is destroyed.

In txiki.js that is a streaming fetch() whose reader is cancelled on the
last chunk of its body while another request to the same origin is
queued behind it: each time, one struct lws stays allocated (and bound to
the client vhost) for the life of the runtime.

Clear close_decided in __lws_wsi_remove_from_sul(), so the flag is only
ever set while the sul_timeout that carries the close out is armed.  Its
other callers are in the close path itself, where the close is already
being carried out and lws_sul_wsitimeout_cb() would have cleared it too.

Not an upstream backport: v5.1.0 (5f39aa483) and main (d954e68f2) have
the same sequence.  It is the deviation txiki.js carried in its backport
of f4a6dc71b/e9008aa7b for the v5.0-stable pin ac23cae70
(lws-close-decided-stands.patch), rebased onto lib/sansio/wsi.c.
Upstream PR: https://github.com/warmcat/libwebsockets/pull/3685

---
diff --git a/lib/sansio/wsi.c b/lib/sansio/wsi.c
index 88995b94f..37070958c 100644
--- a/lib/sansio/wsi.c
+++ b/lib/sansio/wsi.c
@@ -2812,6 +2812,13 @@ lws_callback_as_writeable(struct lws *wsi)
 void
 __lws_wsi_remove_from_sul(struct lws *wsi)
 {
+	/*
+	 * A decided close is carried out by sul_timeout; with it cancelled
+	 * there is nothing left to carry it out, so a later close, eg, the
+	 * KILL_ASYNC on a pipeline leader that handed off its connection, must
+	 * be able to arm it again.
+	 */
+	wsi->close_decided = 0;
 	lws_sul_cancel(&wsi->sul_timeout);
 	lws_sul_cancel(&wsi->sul_hrtimer);
 	lws_sul_cancel(&wsi->sul_validity);
